{
  "title": "Prompts are code: how to measure their value",
  "content": "The answers online were either wrong or skipped the part that mattered. Most measuring platform incidents trace back to a default nobody remembers choosing. Here's the version that skips the happy-path tutorial.\n\n## The problem, in general terms\n\nWhen a growing share of your product's behaviour lives in prompts, tool schemas, and agent graphs rather than in functions and classes, the standard engineering metrics stop telling you much. Lines of code, cyclomatic complexity, test coverage — none of these map cleanly onto a system where the \"logic\" is a paragraph of English and the control flow is a graph of model calls. A founder-engineer looking at the repo can see the diff, but cannot easily answer the question that matters: did this change make the product better, or just different?\n\nThe temptation is to reach for the metrics that already exist. Count tokens. Count prompt versions. Count agent steps. These are easy to instrument and almost useless on their own, because they measure activity rather than value. A prompt that takes twice as many tokens but resolves a support ticket on the first turn is not worse than a terse prompt that loops the user through three clarifications. An agent graph with twelve nodes is not better than one with four — it is just harder to debug at 2am when the client emails.\n\nThe real problem is that prompts and agent graphs have no natural unit of correctness. A function either returns the right value or it does not. A prompt returns a distribution of outputs, and \"right\" depends on context the model cannot see. So any measurement system has to start by deciding what counts as a good outcome, then work backwards to the prompt. This post is about how to build that measurement loop without turning it into a second full-time job.\n\n## The approaches that commonly fail, and why\n\nThe first failure mode is measuring the model instead of the product. Teams commonly set up an eval harness that scores outputs against a golden dataset, watch the score go up, and ship. The score is real, but it is a proxy for a proxy. If the golden dataset was written by the same person who wrote the prompt, it encodes the same blind spots. A prompt can score 95% on a self-authored eval and still fail the first real user who phrases the request in a way the author never imagined.\n\nThe second failure mode is measuring cost in isolation. Token spend is easy to attribute and easy to report, so it becomes the default metric. A prompt that costs half as much per call looks like a win until you notice it is triggering retries, or producing outputs that need human cleanup. Cost per call is a real number, but it is only meaningful next to a completion rate. Optimising cost without holding completion constant is how teams end up with a cheaper system that costs more to operate.\n\nThe third failure mode is treating the agent graph as architecture rather than as a hypothesis. A graph with a planner node, a retrieval node, a critic node, and a synthesis node feels rigorous. It is also four places for the model to hallucinate, four prompts to maintain, and four chances for a timeout to cascade. The graph is a hypothesis about how the task decomposes. If you cannot state the hypothesis in one sentence, the graph is probably compensating for a prompt that is doing too much or too little.\n\nThe fourth failure mode is versioning prompts without versioning the inputs they depend on. A prompt that says \"summarise the document\" behaves differently when the retrieval layer starts returning three chunks instead of one. The prompt did not change, but its behaviour did. Teams that only track prompt versions miss this class of regression entirely.\n\n## The approach that works in practice\n\nThe approach that holds up is to define a small number of outcome metrics that are tied to user-visible behaviour, instrument them at the boundaries of the system, and treat prompts and graphs as implementation details behind those boundaries. This is not novel — it is just normal product instrumentation applied to a new kind of component.\n\nStart by naming the outcomes. For a support agent, that might be: resolved without human handoff, resolved within one turn, and user did not rephrase. For a code assistant, it might be: suggestion accepted, suggestion accepted without edit, and suggestion led to a passing test. For a research agent, it might be: answer cited a source that exists, answer matched the user's question, and answer was delivered within a latency budget. Three to five outcomes is usually enough. More than that and nobody looks at the dashboard.\n\nThen instrument the boundaries. The boundary is wherever your code hands control to the model and gets something back. That is the only place you need to log, because it is the only place where you can observe both the input and the outcome. Everything inside the agent graph — the planner, the critic, the retry loop — is an implementation detail. If you log at the boundary, you can attribute outcomes to the prompt version and the graph version that produced them without instrumenting every node.\n\nFinally, make the prompt and graph versions part of the log record. Not in a comment, not in a git tag, but as a field in the structured log line that also carries the outcome. This is the single change that makes the whole system work, because it lets you answer the question \"did the prompt change help?\" with a query instead of a memory.\n\n## Implementation details\n\nHere is a minimal pattern in Python. It assumes you have some function that calls a model and returns a result, and some way to record whether the result was good. The point is not the specific library — it is the shape of the record.\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport time\nimport uuid\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, asdict\n\n@dataclass\nclass BoundaryRecord:\n    trace_id: str\n    prompt_id: str\n    graph_id: str\n    outcome: str          # \"resolved\", \"handoff\", \"rephrased\"\n    latency_ms: int\n    input_tokens: int\n    output_tokens: int\n    model: str\n\ndef call_with_trace(prompt_id, graph_id, model, messages, outcome_fn):\n    trace_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    start = time.monotonic()\n\n    # This is the boundary. Everything inside is opaque to the metric.\n    result = model.invoke(messages)\n\n    latency_ms = int((time.monotonic() - start) * 1000)\n    outcome = outcome_fn(result)  # returns a string label\n\n    record = BoundaryRecord(\n        trace_id=trace_id,\n        prompt_id=prompt_id,\n        graph_id=graph_id,\n        outcome=outcome,\n        latency_ms=latency_ms,\n        input_tokens=result.usage.input_tokens,\n        output_tokens=result.usage.output_tokens,\n        model=model.name,\n    )\n    log_line = json.dumps(asdict(record))\n    # Write to whatever sink you already have: stdout, a file, a table.\n    print(log_line)\n    return result\n```\n\nThe `outcome_fn` is the interesting part. It is the function that decides what counts as a good result. In practice it is often a small classifier, a regex, or a human review queue. It does not have to be perfect. It has to be consistent, so that changes in the outcome rate reflect changes in the system rather than changes in the judge.\n\nOnce you have these records, the queries you care about are simple. For each prompt_id, what is the outcome distribution? For each graph_id, what is the latency distribution? When prompt_id changed from A to B, did the outcome distribution shift? These are the same queries you would run on any A/B test, because that is what this is.\n\nThe second implementation detail is a prompt registry. You do not need a service. A directory of files with a naming convention is enough.\n\n```\nprompts/\n  support_classify/\n    v1.txt\n    v2.txt\n    current -> v2.txt\n  support_reply/\n    v1.txt\n    current -> v1.txt\n```\n\nThe `current` symlink is what the application reads. When you want to change a prompt, you add a new version and repoint the symlink. The prompt_id in the log record is the directory name plus the version, so you can always map a record back to the exact text that produced it. This is boring, and boring is the point. A prompt registry that requires a deploy to change is a prompt registry nobody uses.\n\nThe third detail is a graph registry, which is the same idea applied to the agent graph. A graph is a set of nodes and edges, and the version is a hash of the graph definition. If you are using a framework that serialises graphs, hash the serialised form. If you are hand-rolling, hash the node names and edges. The hash goes in the log record as `graph_id`. When the graph changes, the hash changes, and you can compare outcomes across hashes.\n\n## What outcomes to expect, and their limits\n\nWith this instrumentation in place, a typical deployment of a small support agent will start to show patterns within a few hundred traces. The most common pattern is that a small number of prompt versions account for most of the variance in outcomes. This is useful because it tells you where to spend your time. The second most common pattern is that latency and outcome are correlated in one direction: faster responses tend to have higher completion rates, up to a point. This is a real effect, but it is easy to over-read. A fast wrong answer is still wrong.\n\nThe limits are worth stating plainly. First, outcome labels are noisy. If your `outcome_fn` is a human reviewer, you have a labelling bottleneck. If it is a classifier, you have a classifier that can drift. Either way, the signal is weaker than a unit test. Treat outcome rates as trends, not as pass/fail gates.\n\nSecond, the instrumentation only covers the boundary you chose. If a failure happens inside the agent graph — a retry loop that eventually succeeds but costs three times as much — the boundary record will show a success. You need a separate cost metric if that matters, and it usually does. The boundary record should carry token counts for this reason.\n\nThird, prompt and graph versions are not independent. Changing the graph changes the effective prompt, because the same prompt text behaves differently when it is called by a planner versus a critic. This means you cannot cleanly attribute an outcome shift to one or the other. The practical answer is to change one thing at a time when you can, and to accept that when you cannot, you are measuring the system, not the component.\n\n## What to watch out for\n\nThe first trap is building the measurement system before you have a product worth measuring. A founder-engineer with ten users does not need a prompt registry. They need to read the ten conversations and fix the obvious problems. The instrumentation described here pays off when you have enough volume that you cannot read every trace, which is usually somewhere in the hundreds to low thousands of interactions per week. Before that, manual review is faster and more accurate.\n\nThe second trap is letting the outcome metric become the goal. If the metric is \"resolved without handoff\", the system will learn to avoid handoff even when handoff is correct. This is the same dynamic as any other metric, and the same defence applies: keep a second metric that measures the thing you are afraid of losing. For a support agent, that might be \"user did not reopen the ticket within seven days\". For a code assistant, \"suggestion accepted without edit\" paired with \"suggestion led to a passing test\".\n\nThe third trap is versioning prompts in a way that makes rollback hard. If the prompt registry is a directory of files, rollback is repointing a symlink. If the prompt registry is a database table edited through an admin UI, rollback is a migration. Prefer the file-based approach until you have a reason not to. The reason not to is usually that non-engineers need to edit prompts, and that is a real reason — but it is a reason to add a UI on top of the files, not to replace them.\n\nThe fourth trap is assuming that a better model will fix a bad prompt. It sometimes will, but the improvement is not predictable, and it does not change the fact that you have no way to tell whether the new model helped. The instrumentation is what lets you answer that question. Without it, a model upgrade is a leap of faith.\n\n## The broader lesson\n\nThe broader lesson is that prompts and agent graphs are code, and like any code they need a way to tell good changes from bad. The unit of measurement is not the prompt or the graph — it is the outcome at the boundary where your system meets the user. Everything else is implementation.\n\nThis is a reversal of the usual instinct. The usual instinct is to measure what is easy: tokens, steps, latency, cost. Those are real and worth watching, but they are inputs, not outcomes. A system that uses more tokens and produces a better result is a better system. A system that uses fewer tokens and produces a worse result is a worse system. The only way to know which you have is to measure the result.\n\nThe second part of the lesson is that the measurement does not have to be sophisticated to be useful. A structured log line with a prompt version and an outcome label, written to a file, queried with a script, is enough to answer most of the questions a founder-engineer actually has. You do not need a platform. You need a consistent record and a willingness to look at it.\n\n## How to apply this to your situation\n\nStart by writing down three outcomes that matter for your product. Not metrics — outcomes. \"The user got an answer they did not have to rephrase.\" \"The suggestion compiled.\" \"The ticket did not come back.\" Then find the one place in your code where the model call returns and the outcome is knowable, and add a log line there with the prompt version and the graph version.\n\nIf you already have logs, add two fields to the existing record: `prompt_id` and `graph_id`. If you do not have logs, add a single `print` statement that emits JSON. The goal is to be able to run one query — \"what is the outcome distribution for prompt version X versus version Y?\" — by the end of the week.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### How do I measure prompt quality without a golden dataset?\n\nYou do not need a golden dataset to start. You need a consistent outcome label. The simplest version is a human review queue: every hundredth trace gets read by you or a teammate, and you record whether the outcome was good. This gives you a noisy but unbiased signal. A golden dataset is useful later, when you want to run automated evals in CI, but it is not the first step. The first step is deciding what \"good\" means and writing it down.\n\n### What is the difference between a prompt version and a graph version?\n\nA prompt version is the text of a single prompt. A graph version is the structure of the agent — which nodes exist, how they are connected, and what each node does. They are separate because they change for different reasons. You change a prompt to fix a wording problem. You change a graph to fix a decomposition problem. Logging both lets you tell which kind of change caused an outcome shift, which is the whole point.\n\n### Why not just use an LLM to grade every output?\n\nYou can, and many teams do. The problem is that the grader is itself a model with its own failure modes, and it can drift as the model behind it is updated. If you use an LLM grader, pin the grader version the same way you pin the prompt version, and periodically spot-check its labels against human review. Treat it as a noisy sensor, not as ground truth.\n\n### When should I stop measuring and just ship?\n\nWhen the cost of measurement exceeds the cost of being wrong. For a pre-product founder, that is almost immediately. For a product with paying users and a support burden, measurement pays for itself quickly. The signal to start is when you find yourself unable to answer \"did that change help?\" from memory. That is the point where a log line is cheaper than a guess.\n\n## Further reading\n\nThe OpenTelemetry documentation covers the general shape of structured traces and spans, which is the same pattern applied to model calls. The OpenTelemetry semantic conventions for generative AI describe a set of standard attribute names for model interactions; adopting them early saves you from inventing your own field names later. For prompt versioning, the pattern described here is close to what feature-flag systems do for code — the same discipline of versioning the thing that changes behaviour, and logging which version was active.\n\n## One thing to do in the next 30 minutes\n\nOpen the file where your application makes its main model call, and add two fields to whatever you log there: a `prompt_id` string and a `graph_id` string. They can be hardcoded for now. The point is to have the fields in place before the next prompt change, so that when you ask \"did that help?\" you have something to query.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
